# id: BM116-wrapper-treats-short-as-fatal
# blinds: the wrapper growing its block buffers instead of failing
# covering-lane: cpp-cgo
# expect: killed
# control-lane: cpp-build
# expect-control: alive
#
# THE FIRST MUTANT IN THIS CATALOGUE THAT PATCHES GO. The runner copies the
# whole tree and applies a patch to it, so it never cared what language the
# file was in; what was missing was a lane that ran the Go side of the boundary,
# and cpp-cgo is that lane. Its control is still cpp-build, because a Go edit
# must leave the C++ build untouched -- if it ever does not, the patch is
# reaching something it has no business reaching.
#
# This is the DEFECT AS IT ACTUALLY OCCURRED, kept rather than invented. The
# wrapper's first version carried a comment asserting that a short buffer was
# "a performance choice and not a correctness one" -- true only of a caller
# that grows, which that version did not do. The parity suite failed on its
# first run against a 100 KB value, iterating.
#
# THE SHAPE IS GF-11's, one boundary out: a comment that states a property the
# code beside it does not implement reads exactly like a comment that describes
# one. The reviewer's eye supplies the missing half. Only a test that crosses
# the buffer's size can tell the two apart, which is why the parity suite keeps
# a value larger than any starting buffer in it permanently.
--- a/engine/riftcgo/iter.go
+++ b/engine/riftcgo/iter.go
@@ -115,7 +115,7 @@
